  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nWe propose a novel method based on a reformulation of the problem using multiple periodic patterns and two-level optimization. Unlike the previous continuous optimization, this approach probabilistically mixes several periodic patterns to generate a step function candidate. The central idea is to recognize the inherent periodic structure in optimal solutions through multiple time scales. The algorithm will search for a harmonic decomposition of the function that minimizes the maximum overlap while maintaining half-integer integral. This differs from the parent approach by explicitly constructing periodic patterns and combining them through a probabilistic mixture formation, leveraging harmonic analysis properties.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Variables: h(x) is represented by a probabilistic mixture of K periodic patterns\n   - Objective: min max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n   - Constraints: \n     * 0\u2264h(x)\u22641\n     * \u222bh(x)dx=0.5\n     * h(x) is a mixture of K periodic patterns: h(x) = \u03a3\u1d62 \u03c9\u1d62 p\u1d62(x) where \u03a3\u03c9\u1d62=1 and p\u1d62 are periodic base functions\n   - Optimization reformulation: \n     Min C subject to \n        C \u2265 \u222b\u2082\u2083 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx for all k \n        \u03a3\u03c9\u1d62=1, 0\u2264\u03c9\u1d62\u22641\n   - Estimators: \n        Use pattern correlations to bound the objective\n        Entropy-based weighting for pattern selection\n        Phase: Initialization of K patterns, formation of mixtures, sequential resolution adjustment\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Step 1: Read parent solution if available (n_parent)\n   - Step 2: Define K_base=3, N_patterns=16 patterns candidate set\n   - Step 3: Generate initial mixture coefficients with simulated annealing ensuring \u03a3\u03c9\u1d62\u22481\n   - Step 4: For each pattern i, precompute its periodic structure (base frequencies, correlations)\n   - Step 5: Calculate log-likelihood of pattern mixture given C threshold\n   - Step 6: Update coefficients using projected gradient with entropy constraint\n   - Step 7: Upsampling strategy: use autocorrelation for scale detection\n   - Step 8: Local search: perturb mixture coefficients with simulated tempering\n   - Step 9: Fallback: store best candidate after each resolution change\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n   - Pattern generation: 5 basic templates\n   - Selection criteria: probabilistic sparsity, minimal correlation with neighbors\n   - Resolution: double parent resolution if C improvement\n   - Stopping criteria: 300s per optimization cycle\n   - Local search frequency: 50% of global iterations\n   - Temporal dynamics: pattern coherence time used for phase validity\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - Primary phase: 25 iterations \u00d7 20 NMS cycles = 500 operations\n   - UPSampling phase: every 10 global iterations\n   - Cycle time: 120s per 20 mixtures (n=256)\n   - Global failure mode: weighted entropy too low\n   - Budget allocation: 45% to core optimization, 15% to pattern resampling, 20% to resolution mapping\n   - Max resolution: min(1024, floor(budget_expent))\n   - Time watchdog: kill process after 5 minutes with best candidate returned\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Feasilibility checked: all h[i]\u2208[0,1], sum=0.5\n   - Objective reevaluation: every 5 global updates\n   - Pattern constraints: auto-correlation at 0 is handeld via squared norm\n   - Conversion failure: use simple dx-sum interpolation on parent solution\n   - Score reporting: return (final_h_values, C5_score, n_points)\n   - Run command: python mixed_patterns_optimization.py --seed=42 --budget=1300\n   - Metrics logging: generate score vs iteration plot\n   - Error handling: abort if entropy coefficient drops below 0.05 while patterns>1",
